      <h2 id="what-is">What compact living really means</h2>
      <p>Compact living is not about squeezing into the smallest box possible. It is about matching your home to how you actually live. Many households rarely use a formal dining room, a guest room or a second living area, yet pay to build, heat, insure and clean them every year. Compact living cuts those spaces and puts the savings into a home that works harder: an open great room, built-in storage, a flex room that changes job, and a porch that adds an outdoor room for very little money.</p>
      <p>It comes in several forms. A <a href="/tiny-house-on-wheels-plans/">tiny house on wheels</a> is usually 120&ndash;300 sq ft plus a loft. A <a href="/tiny-cottage-plans/">tiny cottage</a> or <a href="/small-cabin-plans/">small cabin</a> on a foundation is often 300&ndash;800 sq ft. A backyard <a href="/adu-plans/">ADU</a> puts a compact home on an existing lot. All of them share the same idea, and the same advantages.</p>

